sec.p:
We consider a class of pairwise iSTDP rules triggered by spiking events and purely based on spike-timing difference between pre- and postsynaptic neuron firing, defined by the following expression.

(2)

ontk(pre)→ΔW(pre)=αpre+∑k′<kLΔ−(tk(pre)−tk′(post)),ontk(post)→ΔW(post)=αpost+∑k′<kLΔ+(tk(post)−tk′(pre));
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & \text { on } t_{k}^{(\text {pre })} \rightarrow \Delta W^{(\text {pre })}=\alpha_{\text {pre }}+\sum_{k^{\prime}<k} L^{\Delta_{-}}\left(t_{k}^{(\text {pre })}-t_{k^{\prime}}^{(\text {post })}\right),  \tag{2}\\ & \text { on } t_{k}^{(\text {post })} \rightarrow \Delta W^{(\text {post })}=\alpha_{\text {post }}+\sum_{k^{\prime}<k} L^{\Delta_{+}}\left(t_{k}^{(\text {post })}-t_{k^{\prime}}^{(\text {pre })}\right) ; \end{align*}$$\end{document}



where tk(pre)\begin{document}$t_{k}^{(\mathrm{pre})}$\end{document} and tk(post)\begin{document}$t_{k}^{(\mathrm{post})}$\end{document}represent pre- and postsynaptic spike events, respectively. In our numerical simulations the instantaneous change of weight is further regulated by a global learning rate A0, as in ΔW∗ (pre,post) = A0 ΔW(pre,post) . The scaling by A is omitted in our equations for simplicity. Each presynaptic spike triggers a weight change consisting of a fixed component, denoted αpre, and a pairwise interaction term. Similarly, each postsynaptic spike triggers a weight change with a fixed component αpost and a pairwise interaction term. The pairwise interaction terms capture the cumulative influence of joint spiking events, incorporating the contribution of all preceding spikes (k′ < k) weighted by a kernel LΔ±(⋅)\begin{document}$L^{\Delta_{ \pm}(\cdot)}$\end{document} that depends on the time differences between the spikes. The two functions LΔ−(⋅)\begin{document}$L^{\Delta_{-}}(\cdot)$\end{document} and LΔ+(⋅)\begin{document}$L^{\Delta_{+}(\cdot)}$\end{document} refer to the post-pre and the pre-post pairwise interaction, and are merged in a single function, known as the iSTDP pairwise interaction kernel, as follows:

(3)

L(τ):={LΔ+(τ)ifτ≥0LΔ−(−τ)ifτ<0.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle L(\tau):= \begin{cases}L^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau) & \text { if } \tau \geq 0  \tag{3}\\ L^{\Delta_{-}(-\tau)} & \text { if } \tau<0 .\end{cases}$$\end{document}



By convention, the τ > 0 branch of the curve represents pre-post interactions (presynaptic spike preceding the postsynaptic spike), while the τ < 0 branch represents post-pre interactions (postsynaptic spike preceding the presynaptic spike). In our framework, we consider L(τ) to be independent of αpre and αpost. This is in contrast to previous formulations that incorporate these terms into the kernel representation (e.g., Vogels et al. 2011 and Confavreux et al. 2024). We assume that synaptic plasticity operates on a much slower timescale than neural dynamics, allowing us to treat synaptic weights as constant when calculating first- and second-order firing statistics (Kempter et al. 1999). We further assume variability in the spike-train statistics, so that it can be effectively described as a stochastic process. Thus, we can write the evolution of the mean synaptic weights as:

(4)

d⟨W⟩dt=αprerpre+αpostrpost+Brprerpost+Q,withB:=∫−∞+∞dτL(τ)andQ:=∫−∞+∞dτL(τ)C(τ);
\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned}
&\frac{d\langle W\rangle}{d t}=\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{pre}}+\alpha_{\mathrm{post}} r_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{post}}+Q \quad,\\
&\text { with } B:=\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} d \tau L(\tau) \text { and } Q:=\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} d \tau L(\tau) C(\tau) \text {; }
\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



where B is the integral of the iSTDP kernel, and Q is the only term that depends on the pairwise covariance density between pre and post-synaptic spike trains, here denoted C(τ). For a complete derivation see Supporting Information or Kempter et al. (1999) and Luz and Shamir (2014).

Eq. 4 can be applied to any form of iSTDP kernel, including, e.g., kernels with delayed interactions (Babadi and Abbott 2013). Here, we focus on two specific exponentially decaying shapes: symmetric and antisymmetric, defined as follows:
(5)
Lsy(τ)=12(1τ+A+e−|τ|/τ+−1τ−A−e−|τ|/τ−)andLan(τ)={−A−τ−eτ/τ−ifτ<0A+τ+e−τ/τ+ifτ≥0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle L^{\mathrm{sy}}(\tau)=\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{1}{\tau_{+}} A_{+} e^{-|\tau| / \tau_{+}}-\frac{1}{\tau_{-}} A_{-} e^{-|\tau| / \tau_{-}}\right) \quad \text { and } \quad L^{\mathrm{an}}(\tau)= \begin{cases}-\frac{A_{-}}{\tau_{-}} e^{\tau / \tau_{-}} & \text {if } \tau<0 \tag{5}\\ \frac{A_{+}}{\tau_{+}} e^{-\tau / \tau_{+}} & \text {if } \tau \geq 0\end{cases}$$\end{document}
With this parametrization, A+ = A− implies B = 0, corresponding to covariance-dominated rules, whereas for A− = 0 we have B = A+, a rate-dominated regime. This choice of L(τ) allows us to keep track of the entire spiking history through activity traces updated at every spike time and decaying in time with the same constants as the iSTDP kernel. These traces will determine the amount of potentiation (+) or depression (−) triggered by each spike. For the antisymmetric rule, the presynaptic and postsynaptic traces are respectively defined as follows:
(6)
ddtTr+(t)=−1τ+Tr+(t)andddtTo−(t)=−1τ−To−(t)update:{Tr+(t+ε)=Tr+(t)+1To−(t+ε)ont=to−(t)+1ont=t(pre) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & \frac{d}{d t} T_{r+}(t)=-\frac{1}{\tau_{+}} T_{r+}(t) \quad \text { and } \quad \frac{d}{d t} T_{o-}(t)=-\frac{1}{\tau_{-}} T_{o-}(t) \\ & \text { update: } \quad\left\{\begin{array}{ll} T_{r+}(t+\varepsilon) & =T_{r+}(t)+1 \\ T_{o-}(t+\varepsilon) & \text { on } t=t_{o-}(t)+1 \\ \end{array} \text { on } t=t^{(\mathrm{pre})}\right. \tag{6} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
The symmetric rule requires, in addition to Tr+(t) and T0−(t), the traces Tr−(t) and T0+ (t), defined analogously. The implementation of the asymmetric iSTDP rule is then given by:
(7)
{ΔWan(t)=αpre−A−τA¯+To−(t)ont=t(pre)ΔWan(t)=αpost+A¯+τ+Tr+(t)ont=t(post), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \left\{\begin{array}{l} \Delta W^{\mathrm{an}}(t)=\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}}-\frac{A_{-}}{\tau_{\bar{A}_{+}}} T_{o-}(t) \quad \text { on } t=t^{(\mathrm{pre})} \tag{7}\\ \Delta W^{\mathrm{an}}(t)=\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+\frac{\bar{A}_{+}}{\tau_{+}} T_{r+}(t) \quad \text { on } t=t^{(\mathrm{post})}, \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}
and for the symmetric rule:
(8)
{ΔWsy(t)=αpre+A+2τ+To+(t)−A−2τ−To−(t)ont=t(pre)ΔWsy(t)=αpost+A+2τ+Tr+(t)−A−2τ−Tr−(t)ont=t(post). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \left\{\begin{array}{l} \Delta W^{\mathrm{sy}}(t)=\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}}+\frac{A_{+}}{2 \tau_{+}} T_{o+}(t)-\frac{A_{-}}{2 \tau_{-}} T_{o-}(t) \quad \text { on } t=t^{(\mathrm{pre})} \tag{8}\\ \Delta W^{\mathrm{sy}}(t)=\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+\frac{A_{+}}{2 \tau_{+}} T_{r+}(t)-\frac{A_{-}}{2 \tau_{-}} T_{r-}(t) \quad \text { on } t=t^{(\mathrm{post})} . \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}

Finding a general solution for Eq. 4 can be challenging. For our purposes, we consider a simplified case. First, we model an excitatory and an inhibitory neuron as mutually interacting Poisson processes (Hawkes 1971; Ocker et al. 2017). Each time the presynaptic neuron spikes, the firing probability of the postsynaptic neuron increases (or decreases) proportionally to the excitatory (or inhibitory) post-synaptic potential EPSP (IPSP) kernel:
(9)
Ee(τ)=1τee−τ/τeandEi(τ)=−1τie−τ/τiwithτ≥0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle E_e(\tau)=\frac{1}{\tau_e} e^{-\tau / \tau_e} \quad \text { and } \quad E_i(\tau)=-\frac{1}{\tau_i} e^{-\tau / \tau_i} \quad \text { with } \tau \geq 0$$\end{document}
We further assume that the input to the inhibitory neuron hinh(t) is regulated homeostatically to keep its rate rinhconstant. Namely,
(10)
hinh(t)=rinh−hexcwexc+rinhwexcwinh(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} h_{\mathrm{inh}}(t)=r_{\mathrm{inh}}-h_{\mathrm{exc}} w_{\mathrm{exc}}+r_{\mathrm{inh}} w_{\mathrm{exc}} w_{\mathrm{inh}}(t) . \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
In those conditions, Eq. 4 has an approximate fixed-point closed-form solution for the inhibitory connection winh:
(11)
w~inh=αprerinh+hexcΨrinh(Ψ−Mei(10));r~exc=−αprerinh+Mei(10)hexcΨ−Mei(10);withΨ=αpost+Brinh+Mie(01)wexc. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gather*} \tilde{w}_{\mathrm{inh}}=\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}+h_{\mathrm{exc}} \Psi}{r_{\mathrm{inh}}\left(\Psi-M_{e i}^{(10)}\right)} ; \quad \tilde{r}_{\mathrm{exc}}=-\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}+M_{e i}^{(10)} h_{\mathrm{exc}}}{\Psi-M_{e i}^{(10)}} ; \tag{11}\\ \text { with } \Psi=\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{inh}}+M_{i e}^{(01)} w_{\mathrm{exc}} . \end{gather*}$$\end{document}
The coefficients Mei(10)\begin{document}$M_{e i}^{(10)}$\end{document} and Mie(01)\begin{document}$M_{i e}^{(01)}$\end{document} are found through the linear response theory (Hawkes 1971; Ocker et al. 2017), and depend on the iSTDP kernel and the EPSP / IPSP parameters (refer to the analytic derivation of the SI Text, Section S1.4).

The ring model was implemented with the same neural dynamics as the random network (Eq. 14). Here however both excitatory and inhibitory neurons received a baseline external input, independent for each unit. We used 800 excitatory units and 200 inhibitory units, further divided into a PV and an SST subclass. The synaptic weight between an excitatory neuron and its neighbors decayed with distance, following a bell-shaped curve modeled as a rescaled von Mises distribution:

(15)

wij(∙−e):=C∙←e(exp⁡(k∙←ecos⁡(θi−θj)));
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle w_{i j}^{(\bullet-\mathrm{e})}:=C_{\bullet \leftarrow \mathrm{e}}\left(\exp \left(k_{\bullet \leftarrow \mathrm{e}} \cos \left(\theta_i-\theta_j\right)\right)\right) ;$$\end{document}



where θi and θj represent the locations of the post- and presynaptic neuron on the ring, with each population equally spaced in the [0, 2π) interval. The factor k·←e regulates the connection range. Finally the scaling factor sum w·←e. All inh-to-inh connections were fixed and dense. We considered two distinct inhibitory populations C·←e is tuned so that the sum of incoming weight+-s to either exc, PV or SST population is at a desired value with identical dynamics, but different iSTDP rules. The first population had a symmetric iSTDP kernel, the second an antisymmetric one. The input was different than in the previous model, with a constant external current in the excitatory population, and independent excitatory spike trains for all units.

It is useful to express each spike train as a sum of Dirac delta functions centered at each spike time:
(S3)
Si(t):=∑kδ(t−tki) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} S^{i}(t):=\sum_{k} \delta\left(t-t_{k}^{i}\right) \tag{S3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where i denotes the neuron index. With this notation, the number of spikes in the [ta, tb] interval is:
(S4)
N[ta,tb]i=∫tatbdtSi(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} N_{\left[t_{a}, t_{b}\right]}^{i}=\int_{t_{a}}^{t_{b}} d t S^{i}(t) \tag{S4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Assuming stationary conditions, we can therefore define the firing rate of neuron i as:

(S5)

ri:=1T∫0TdtSi(t)=⟨Si(t)⟩t
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{i}:=\frac{1}{T} \int_{0}^{T} d t S^{i}(t)=\left\langle S^{i}(t)\right\rangle_{t} \tag{S5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



Using the definition in Eq. S3, we can then rewrite the pairwise interaction terms in Eq. S1 as:

(S6)

∑k′LΔ±(t−tk′i)=∫−∞tdτSi(τ)LΔ±(t−τ)=∫0∞dτ∗Si(t−τ∗)LΔ±(τ∗)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \sum_{k^{\prime}} L^{\Delta_{ \pm}}\left(t-t_{k^{\prime}}^{i}\right)=\int_{-\infty}^{t} d \tau S^{i}(\tau) L^{\Delta_{ \pm}}(t-\tau)=\int_{0}^{\infty} d \tau^{*} S^{i}\left(t-\tau^{*}\right) L^{\Delta_{ \pm}}\left(\tau^{*}\right) \tag{S6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



Where in the last step we performed the change of variable τ ←t − τ ∗. This leads to the following expression for the instantaneous change in synaptic weights, numerically equivalent to Eq. S1:

(S7)

ont=tk(pre)→ΔW(pre)(t)=αpre+∫0∞dτSpost(t−τ)LΔ−(τ)ont=tk(post)→ΔW(post)(t)=αpost+∫0∞dτSpre(t−τ)LΔ+(τ)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & \text { on } t=t_{k}^{(\text {pre })} \rightarrow \Delta W^{(\text {pre })}(t)=\alpha_{\text {pre }}+\int_{0}^{\infty} d \tau S^{\text {post }}(t-\tau) L^{\Delta_{-}}(\tau)  \tag{S7}\\ & \text { on } t=t_{k}^{(\text {post })} \rightarrow \Delta W^{(\text {post })}(t)=\alpha_{\text {post }}+\int_{0}^{\infty} d \tau S^{\text {pre }}(t-\tau) L^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau) \end{align*}$$\end{document}



Now, using again the same definition of spike train in Eq. S3, an event-based update can be expressed as an integral over time over the pre- and post-synaptic spiking events. The total weight change occurring in an interval [ta, tb] is:

(S8)

ΔW[ta,tb]=∫tatbdtSpre(t)ΔW(pre)(t)+∫tatbSpost(t)ΔW(post)(t)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Delta W_{\left[t_{a}, t_{b}\right]}=\int_{t_{a}}^{t_{b}} d t S^{\mathrm{pre}}(t) \Delta W^{(\mathrm{pre})}(t)+\int_{t_{a}}^{t_{b}} S^{\mathrm{post}}(t) \Delta W^{(\mathrm{post})}(t) \tag{S8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



We can then express the average weight change per second, considering a time interval [0, T ] large enough to ensure precise sampling in second-order firing statistics, but still small compared to the rate of changes in ΔW. Under this assumption of separation of timescales between neuronal dynamics and plasticity, we can substitute Eq. S7 into Eq. S8, and write:

(S9)

⟨ΔW(t)⟩t=1T∫0TdtSpre(t)αpre+1T∫0TdtSpost(t)αpost++1T∫0Tdt∫0∞dτSpost(t)Spre(t−τ)LΔ+(τ)+1T∫0Tdt∫0∞dτSpre(t)Spost(t−τ)LΔ−(τ)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gather*} \langle\Delta W(t)\rangle_{t}=\frac{1}{T} \int_{0}^{T} d t S^{\text {pre }}(t) \alpha_{\text {pre }}+\frac{1}{T} \int_{0}^{T} d t S^{\text {post }}(t) \alpha_{\text {post }}+  \tag{S9}\\ +\frac{1}{T} \int_{0}^{T} d t \int_{0}^{\infty} d \tau S^{\text {post }}(t) S^{\text {pre }}(t-\tau) L^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau)+\frac{1}{T} \int_{0}^{T} d t \int_{0}^{\infty} d \tau S^{\text {pre }}(t) S^{\text {post }}(t-\tau) L^{\Delta_{-}}(\tau) \end{gather*}$$\end{document}



Since αpre and αpost are constants, we can factor them out. The first two terms of the r.h.s. of Eq. S9 become:

(S10)

1T∫0TdtSpre(t)αpre=rpreαpreand1T∫0TdtSpost(t)αpost=rpostαpost
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{1}{T} \int_{0}^{T} d t S^{\mathrm{pre}}(t) \alpha_{\mathrm{pre}}=r_{\mathrm{pre}} \alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} \quad \text { and } \quad \frac{1}{T} \int_{0}^{T} d t S^{\mathrm{post}}(t) \alpha_{\mathrm{post}}=r_{\mathrm{post}} \alpha_{\mathrm{post}} \tag{S10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



The pairwise interaction terms instead can be simplified as follows:

(S11)

1T∫0Tdt∫0∞dτSpost(t)Spre(t−τ)LΔ+(τ)=rprerpost∫0∞dτLΔ+(τ)+∫0∞dτCΔ+(τ)LΔ+(τ)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{1}{T} \int_{0}^{T} d t \int_{0}^{\infty} d \tau S^{\mathrm{post}}(t) S^{\mathrm{pre}}(t-\tau) L^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau)=r_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{post}} \int_{0}^{\infty} d \tau L^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau)+\int_{0}^{\infty} d \tau C^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau) L^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau) \tag{S11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



and similarly for the last term in Eq. S9. In the above equation, CΔ+(τ)\begin{document}$C^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau)$\end{document} denotes the covariance density between the pre- and the postsynaptic neuron, following the convention that Δ+ represents the pre-post order and Δ− the post-pre order:

(S12)

CΔ+(τ):=1T∫0T(Spost(t)−rpost)(Spre(t−τ)−rpre).
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau):=\frac{1}{T} \int_{0}^{T}\left(S^{\mathrm{post}}(t)-r_{\mathrm{post}}\right)\left(S^{\mathrm{pre}}(t-\tau)-r_{\mathrm{pre}}\right) . \tag{S12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



An analogous definition holds for CΔ−(τ)\begin{document}$C^{\Delta_{-}}(\tau)$\end{document}, with the pre- and post-temporal order inverted. Therefore

(S13)

ΔW(t)⟩t=rpreαpre+rpostαpost+rprerpost∫0∞dτ(LΔ−(τ)+LΔ+(τ))++∫0∞dτCΔ−(τ)LΔ−(τ)+∫0∞dτCΔ+(τ)LΔ+(τ)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \Delta W(t)\rangle_{t}= & r_{\text {pre }} \alpha_{\text {pre }}+r_{\text {post }} \alpha_{\text {post }}+r_{\text {pre }} r_{\text {post }} \int_{0}^{\infty} d \tau\left(L^{\Delta_{-}}(\tau)+L^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau)\right)+  \tag{S13}\\ & +\int_{0}^{\infty} d \tau C^{\Delta_{-}}(\tau) L^{\Delta_{-}}(\tau)+\int_{0}^{\infty} d \tau C^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau) L^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau) \end{align*}$$\end{document}



Now using Eq. S2, we can write:

(S14)

rprerpost∫0∞dτ(LΔ−(τ)+LΔ+(τ))=rprerpost∫−∞∞dτL(τ);
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{\text {pre }} r_{\text {post }} \int_{0}^{\infty} d \tau\left(L^{\Delta_{-}}(\tau)+L^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau)\right)=r_{\text {pre }} r_{\text {post }} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} d \tau L(\tau) ; \tag{S14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



and given that CΔ+(τ)=CΔ−(−τ)\begin{document}$C^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau)=C^{\Delta_{-}}(-\tau)$\end{document} we can perform the following variable substitution:

(S15)

∫0∞dτCΔ−(τ)LΔ−(τ)=∫−∞0dτCΔ+(τ)LΔ+(τ)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \int_{0}^{\infty} d \tau C^{\Delta_{-}}(\tau) L^{\Delta_{-}}(\tau)=\int_{-\infty}^{0} d \tau C^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau) L^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau) \tag{S15} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



Finally, substituting Eq. S14 and Eq. S15 in Eq. S13, and defining C(τ):=CΔ+(τ)\begin{document}$C(\tau):=C^{\Delta_{+}}(\tau)$\end{document}, we have:

(S16)

⟨ΔW(t)⟩t=αprerpre+αpostrpost+Brprerpost+QwithB:=∫−∞+∞dτL(τ)andQ:=∫−∞+∞dτL(τ)C(τ);
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned}
&\langle\Delta W(t)\rangle_t=\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{pre}}+\alpha_{\mathrm{post}} r_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{post}}+Q\\
&\text { with } B:=\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} d \tau L(\tau) \text { and } Q:=\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} d \tau L(\tau) C(\tau) \text {; }
\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



Corresponding to Eq. 1 in the main text.

In this section, we derive a closed-form solution for a reduced model of one inhibitory and one excitatory neuron (Eq. S16). We model the two neurons as mutually interacting Hawkes processes (Main Text, Fig. 1A, Hawkes 1971; Pernice et al. 2011). The excitatory unit receives a constant input current hexc, a plastic inhibitory connection winh(t) and it projects a fixed excitatory connection wexc to the inhibitory neuron. For simplicity, we assume that the inhibitory unit is kept at a constant rate rinh by an adaptive external current hinh(t) (10). In this framework, the average firing rates behave linearly, therefore, the excitatory rate can be written as:

(S17)

rexc(t)=hexc−winh(t)rinh.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{\mathrm{exc}}(t)=h_{\mathrm{exc}}-w_{\mathrm{inh}}(t) r_{\mathrm{inh}} . \tag{S17} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



As we focus on the connection winh(t) from the inhibitory to the excitatory neuron, we rename rinh = rpre and rexc = rpost in Eq. S16 and we get:

(S18)

⟨dwinh(t)dt⟩=αprerinh+αpost(hexc−winhrinh)+Brinh(hexc−winhrinh)+Q=αprerinh−winhrinh(αpost+Brinh)+hexc(αpost+Brinh)+Q.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gather*} \left\langle\frac{d w_{\mathrm{inh}}(t)}{d t}\right\rangle=\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}+\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}\left(h_{\mathrm{exc}}-w_{\mathrm{inh}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}\right)+B r_{\mathrm{inh}}\left(h_{\mathrm{exc}}-w_{\mathrm{inh}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}\right)+Q  \tag{S18}\\ =\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}-w_{\mathrm{inh}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}\left(\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{inh}}\right)+h_{\mathrm{exc}}\left(\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{inh}}\right)+Q . \end{gather*}$$\end{document}



The only nontrivial quantity to compute is the term Q, as it requires the inhibitory-to-excitatory covariance density curve. To avoid ambiguities, we define the Fourier transform operator acting on a function f (t) and the convolution operator acting on two functions f (t) and g(t) as follows:

(S19)

f^(ω)=∫−∞+∞dtf(t)e−jωtand(f∗g)(τ)=∫−∞+∞dtf(t)g(τ−t);
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \widehat{f}(\omega)=\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} d t \quad f(t) e^{-j \omega t} \quad \text { and } \quad(f * g)(\tau)=\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} d t \quad f(t) g(\tau-t) ; \tag{S19} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



where j denotes the imaginary unit.

Note that the total integral Q of the product C(τ) L(τ) (as defined in Eq. S16) is equivalent to its Fourier transform evaluated at ω = 0. But for the inverse convolution theorem, the Fourier transform of the product C(τ) L(τ) corresponds to the convolution of the two transforms C( ω) and L^(ω)\begin{document}$\widehat{L}(\omega)$\end{document} . In equations:
(S20)
Q=∫−∞∞dτC(τ)L(τ)=f^(C(τ)L(τ))(ω)|ω=0=12π(C^(ω~)∗L^(ω~))(ω)|ω=0==12π∫−∞∞dω~C^(ω~)L^(−ω~) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} Q=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} d \tau C(\tau) L(\tau)= & \left.\hat{f}(C(\tau) L(\tau))(\omega)\right|_{\omega=0}=\left.\frac{1}{2 \pi}(\hat{C}(\tilde{\omega}) * \hat{L}(\tilde{\omega}))(\omega)\right|_{\omega=0}= \tag{S20}\\ & =\frac{1}{2 \pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} d \tilde{\omega} \hat{C}(\tilde{\omega}) \hat{L}(-\tilde{\omega}) \end{align*}$$\end{document}
The advantage of re-defining Q in the Fourier space is that, for Hawkes processes, the covariance C( ω) can be expressed in a closed form as a function of network connectivity and neural activity (Hawkes 1971; Pernice et al. 2011; Trousdale et al. 2012; Jovanović et al. 2015). More specifically, given the matrix of post-synaptic potential kernels (Eq. 9 in Methods)
(S21)
E(τ)=(1τee−τ/τe00−1τie−τ/τi) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathbf{E}(\tau)=\left(\begin{array}{cc} \frac{1}{\tau_{e}} e^{-\tau / \tau_{e}} & 0 \tag{S21}\\ 0 & -\frac{1}{\tau_{i}} e^{-\tau / \tau_{i}} \end{array}\right)$$\end{document}
and the connectivity matrix
(S22)
W=(0winhwexc0), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathbf{W}=\left(\begin{array}{cc} 0 & w_{\mathrm{inh}} \tag{S22}\\ w_{\mathrm{exc}} & 0 \end{array}\right),$$\end{document}
we can first write the vector of firing rates r = [rexc, rinh]⊺ as
(S23)
r=h0+∫−∞+∞dτG(τ)S(τ−t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r=\mathbf{h}_{0}+\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} d \tau \mathbf{G}(\tau) S(\tau-t) \tag{S23} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where h0 is the baseline input and G(τ) = E(τ) W is referred to as the matrix of “interaction kernels”. At the equilibrium state,
(S24)
r=h0+Gr,withG:=∫−∞+∞dτG(τ) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r=\mathbf{h}_{0}+\mathbf{G} \mathbf{r}, \quad \text { with } \mathbf{G}:=\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} d \tau \mathbf{G}(\tau) \tag{S24} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where the integral in the r.h.s. corresponds to the Fourier transform evaluated at zero, i.e., Ĝ( ω)|ω=0. Equivalently, the rate vector can be written in an explicit form:
(S25)
r=(I−G)−1h0, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r=(\mathbf{I}-\mathbf{G})^{-1} \mathbf{h}_{\mathbf{0}}, \tag{S25} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where I represents the identity matrix. Moreover, if we write the covariance matrix (cf. Eq. S12) as
(S26)
C(τ)=⟨S(t)S(t−τ)T⟩−rrT \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{C}(\tau)=\left\langle\mathbf{S}(t) \mathbf{S}(t-\tau)^{T}\right\rangle-\mathbf{r r}^{T} \tag{S26} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
then we have the following expression in the Fourier domain, at the equilibrium state:
(S27)
C^(ω)=(I−G^(ω))−1D(I−G^T(ω))−1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{\mathbf{C}}(\omega)=(\mathbf{I}-\hat{\mathbf{G}}(\omega))^{-1} \mathbf{D}\left(\mathbf{I}-\hat{\mathbf{G}}^{T}(\omega)\right)^{-1} \tag{S27} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where D is a diagonal matrix with the firing rates on its diagonal. Finally, recall that, for a matrix G with spectral radius < 1,
(S28)
I−G)−1=∑α∞Gα. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{I}-\mathbf{G})^{-1}=\sum_{\alpha}^{\infty} \mathbf{G}^{\alpha} . \tag{S28} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Therefore, the covariance matrix can be written as the following expansion
(S29)
C^(ω)=∑α=0N∑β=0NE^α(ω)(E^T)β(−ω)[(Wα)D(WT)β)], \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left.\hat{\mathbf{C}}(\omega)=\sum_{\alpha=0}^{N} \sum_{\beta=0}^{N} \hat{\mathbf{E}}^{\alpha}(\omega)\left(\hat{\mathbf{E}}^{T}\right)^{\beta}(-\omega)\left[\left(\mathbf{W}^{\alpha}\right) \mathbf{D}\left(\mathbf{W}^{T}\right)^{\beta}\right)\right], \tag{S29} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where the indices α, β indicate the number of connections between any spiking neuron in the network to the post- and to the presynaptic neuron, respectively. This captures the influence of each spike propagated in the network through existing synaptic connections on the correlated firing of the pre- and postsynaptic neurons. Within this formalism, in our reduced model we consider only pairwise interactions, that is, α + β = 1. The inhibitory-to-excitatory covariance is the (1, 2) element of Ĉ( ω). We can compute it as:
(S30)
C^(ω)=E^e(ω)winhrinh+E^i(−ω)rexcwexc \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{C}(\omega)=\hat{E}_{e}(\omega) w_{\mathrm{inh}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}+\hat{E}_{i}(-\omega) r_{\mathrm{exc}} w_{\mathrm{exc}} \tag{S30} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
By combining Eq. S20 and Eq. S30, we get the following expression for Q:
(S31)
Q=Mei(10)winhrinh+Mie(01)wexcrexcMjk(αβ):=∫−∞+∞dωE^jα(ω)E^kβ(−ω)L^(−ω) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & Q=M_{e i}^{(10)} w_{\mathrm{inh}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}+M_{i e}^{(01)} w_{\mathrm{exc}} r_{\mathrm{exc}} \\ & M_{j k}^{(\alpha \beta)}:=\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} d \omega \hat{E}_{j}^{\alpha}(\omega) \hat{E}_{k}^{\beta}(-\omega) \hat{L}(-\omega) \tag{S31} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
The Mei(10)\begin{document}$M_{e i}^{(10)}$\end{document}, Mie(01)\begin{document}$M_{i e}^{(01)}$\end{document} coefficients can be computed analytically depending on the iSTDP rule (see next section). Considering them as constants, we can then write Eq. S18 as:
(S32)
⟨dwinh(t)dt⟩=αprerinh−winhrinh(αpost+Brinh)+hexc(αpost+Brinh)+Mei(10)winhrinh+Mie(01)wexcrexc \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gather*} \left\langle\frac{d w_{\mathrm{inh}}(t)}{d t}\right\rangle=\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}-w_{\mathrm{inh}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}\left(\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{inh}}\right)+h_{\mathrm{exc}}\left(\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{inh}}\right)+ \tag{S32}\\ M_{e i}^{(10)} w_{\mathrm{inh}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}+M_{i e}^{(01)} w_{\mathrm{exc}} r_{\mathrm{exc}} \end{gather*}$$\end{document}
or equivalently,
(S33)
⟨dwinh(t)dt⟩=αprerinh−winhrinh(Ψ−Mei(10))+hexcΨ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left\langle\frac{d w_{\mathrm{inh}}(t)}{d t}\right\rangle=\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}-w_{\mathrm{inh}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}\left(\Psi-M_{e i}^{(10)}\right)+h_{\mathrm{exc}} \Psi \tag{S33} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
with Ψ ∶= αpost + Brinh + M(01)wexc.

We then find the stationary point (Eq. 11 in Methods)
(S34)
w~inh=αprerinh+hexcΨrinh(Ψ−Mei(10)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tilde{w}_{\mathrm{inh}}=\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}+h_{\mathrm{exc}} \Psi}{r_{\mathrm{inh}}\left(\Psi-M_{e i}^{(10)}\right)} \tag{S34} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The equilibrium point w~inh\begin{document}$\tilde{w}_{\text {inh }}$\end{document} is a stable fixed point attractor, and positive, that is w~inh∈[0,wmax]\begin{document}$\tilde{w}_{\text {inh }} \in\left[0, w_{\max }\right]$\end{document}, if the following conditions hold:
(S35)
Ψ−Mei(10)>0αprerinh+Ψhexc>0, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \Psi-M_{e i}^{(10)} & >0 \tag{S35}\\ \alpha_{\text {pre }} r_{\text {inh }}+\Psi h_{\text {exc }} & >0, \end{align*}$$\end{document}
If a solution for w~inh\begin{document}$\tilde{w}_{\text {inh }}$\end{document} exists, we can also express the steady-state postsynaptic rate as follows:
(S36)
r~exc=hexc−w~inhrinh=−αprerinh+Mei(10)hexcΨ−Mei(10), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tilde{r}_{\mathrm{exc}}=h_{\mathrm{exc}}-\tilde{w}_{\mathrm{inh}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}=-\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}+M_{e i}^{(10)} h_{\mathrm{exc}}}{\Psi-M_{e i}^{(10)}}, \tag{S36} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The solution is valid only for positive rates. This adds one more condition for a stable and positive solution. The constraints are therefore:
(S37)
Ψ−Mei(10)>0αprerinh+Ψhexc>0rinh>Mei(10)hexc−αprewithαpre<0. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gather*} \Psi-M_{e i}^{(10)}>0 \\ \alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}+\Psi h_{\mathrm{exc}}>0 \tag{S37}\\ r_{\mathrm{inh}}>\frac{M_{e i}^{(10)} h_{\mathrm{exc}}}{-\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}}} \text { with } \alpha_{\mathrm{pre}}<0 . \end{gather*}$$\end{document}

Consider the model shown in Fig. S3A. Here both rexc and rinh are free parameters, given by the net input. Namely:
(S39)
rexc=hexc−winhrinhrinh=hinh+wexcrexc \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & r_{\mathrm{exc}}=h_{\mathrm{exc}}-w_{\mathrm{inh}} r_{\mathrm{inh}} \tag{S39}\\ & r_{\mathrm{inh}}=h_{\mathrm{inh}}+w_{\mathrm{exc}} r_{\mathrm{exc}} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
where we dropped the time dependence in the notation for simplicity. We can rewrite the equations above as:
(S40)
rexc=hexc−winhhinh1+wexcwinh;rinh=hinh+wexchexc1+wexcwinh. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{\mathrm{exc}}=\frac{h_{\mathrm{exc}}-w_{\mathrm{inh}} h_{\mathrm{inh}}}{1+w_{\mathrm{exc}} w_{\mathrm{inh}}} ; \quad r_{\mathrm{inh}}=\frac{h_{\mathrm{inh}}+w_{\mathrm{exc}} h_{\mathrm{exc}}}{1+w_{\mathrm{exc}} w_{\mathrm{inh}}} . \tag{S40} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Then, plugging the terms above into (S32), we obtain:
(S41)
⟨dwinh(t)dt⟩=αprehinh+wexchexc1+wexcwinh−winhhinh+wexchexc1+wexcwinh(αpost+Bhinh+wexchexc1+wexcwinh)+hexc(αpost+Bhinh+wexchexc1+wexcwinh)+Mei(10)winhhinh+wexchexc1+wexcwinh+Mie(01)wexchexc−winhhinh1+wexcwinh. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \left\langle\frac{d w_{\mathrm{inh}}(t)}{d t}\right\rangle= & \alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} \frac{h_{\mathrm{inh}}+w_{\mathrm{exc}} h_{\mathrm{exc}}}{1+w_{\mathrm{exc}} w_{\mathrm{inh}}}-w_{\mathrm{inh}} \frac{h_{\mathrm{inh}}+w_{\mathrm{exc}} h_{\mathrm{exc}}}{1+w_{\mathrm{exc}} w_{\mathrm{inh}}}\left(\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B \frac{h_{\mathrm{inh}}+w_{\mathrm{exc}} h_{\mathrm{exc}}}{1+w_{\mathrm{exc}} w_{\mathrm{inh}}}\right)+ \\ & h_{\mathrm{exc}}\left(\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B \frac{h_{\mathrm{inh}}+w_{\mathrm{exc}} h_{\mathrm{exc}}}{1+w_{\mathrm{exc}} w_{\mathrm{inh}}}\right)+M_{e i}^{(10)} w_{\mathrm{inh}} \frac{h_{\mathrm{inh}}+w_{\mathrm{exc}} h_{\mathrm{exc}}}{1+w_{\mathrm{exc}} w_{\mathrm{inh}}}+ \tag{S41}\\ & M_{i e}^{(01)} w_{\mathrm{exc}} \frac{h_{\mathrm{exc}}-w_{\mathrm{inh}} h_{\mathrm{inh}}}{1+w_{\mathrm{exc}} w_{\mathrm{inh}}} . \end{align*}$$\end{document}
We can find a fixed point w~inh\begin{document}$\tilde{w}_{\text {inh }}$\end{document} in the equation above by setting ⟨dwinh(t)dt⟩=0\begin{document}$\left\langle\frac{d w_{\operatorname{inh}}(t)}{d t}\right\rangle=0$\end{document} and multiplying by (1 + wexc winh)2. The equation becomes a quadratic equation for winh:
(S42)
awinh2+bwinh+c=0, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} a w_{\mathrm{inh}}^{2}+b w_{\mathrm{inh}}+c=0, \tag{S42} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
With
(S43)
a=wexc(Mei(10)(hinh+hexcwexc)−Mie(01)hinhwexc−αposthinh)b=(hinh+hexcwexc)(αprewexc−Bhinh+Mei(10))−αposthinh(1−hexcwexc)+Mie(01)wexc(hexcwexc−hinh)c=(hinh+hexcwexc)(αpre+Bhexc)+Mie(01)wexchexc+αposthexc \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & a=w_{\text {exc }}\left(M_{e i}^{(10)}\left(h_{\text {inh }}+h_{\text {exc }} w_{\text {exc }}\right)-M_{i e}^{(01)} h_{\text {inh }} w_{\text {exc }}-\alpha_{\text {post }} h_{\text {inh }}\right) \\ & b=\left(h_{\text {inh }}+h_{\text {exc }} w_{\text {exc }}\right)\left(\alpha_{\text {pre }} w_{\text {exc }}-B h_{\text {inh }}+M_{e i}^{(10)}\right)-\alpha_{\text {post }} h_{\text {inh }}\left(1-h_{\text {exc }} w_{\text {exc }}\right)+M_{i e}^{(01)} w_{\text {exc }}\left(h_{\text {exc }} w_{\text {exc }}-h_{\text {inh }}\right) \\ & c=\left(h_{\text {inh }}+h_{\text {exc }} w_{\text {exc }}\right)\left(\alpha_{\text {pre }}+B h_{\text {exc }}\right)+M_{i e}^{(01)} w_{\text {exc }} h_{\text {exc }}+\alpha_{\text {post }} h_{\text {exc }} \tag{S43} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
The solution is then given by
(S44)
w~inh=−b+b2−4ac2a \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tilde{w}_{\text {inh }}=\frac{-b+\sqrt{b^{2}-4 a c}}{2 a} \tag{S44} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
as long as b2 − 4ac ≥ 0.

Consider first the Hebbian symmetric STDP kernel, defined via the function
(S47)
LSy(τ)=12(1τ+A+e−|τ|/τ+−1τ−A−e|τ|/τ−) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} L^{\mathrm{Sy}}(\tau)=\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{1}{\tau_{+}} A_{+} e^{-|\tau| / \tau_{+}}-\frac{1}{\tau_{-}} A_{-} e^{|\tau| / \tau_{-}}\right) \tag{S47} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
with A+, A− > 0. The Fourier transform is given by
(S48)
L^sy(ω)=L^sy(−ω)=A+1+τ+2ω2−A−1+τ−2ω2. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{L}^{\mathrm{sy}}(\omega)=\hat{L}^{\mathrm{sy}}(-\omega)=\frac{A_{+}}{1+\tau_{+}^{2} \omega^{2}}-\frac{A_{-}}{1+\tau_{-}^{2} \omega^{2}} . \tag{S48} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
By substituting Eq. S46 and Eq. S48 in Eq. S45, we can calculate the first-order motif coefficients Meisy,(10)\begin{document}$M_{e i}^{\mathrm{sy},(10)}$\end{document}, Miesy,(01)\begin{document}$M_{i e}^{\mathrm{sy},(01)}$\end{document}:
(S49)
Meisy,(10)=A+τ++τi−A−τ−+τi;Miesy,(01)=A+τ++τe−A−τ−+τe, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} M_{e i}^{\mathrm{sy},(10)}=\frac{A_{+}}{\tau_{+}+\tau_{i}}-\frac{A_{-}}{\tau_{-}+\tau_{i}} \quad ; \quad M_{i e}^{\mathrm{sy},(01)}=\frac{A_{+}}{\tau_{+}+\tau_{e}}-\frac{A_{-}}{\tau_{-}+\tau_{e}}, \tag{S49} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and therefore find an analytical solution for the integral Q in Eq. S31. Consider now the antisymmetric iSTDP rule, with kernel:
(S50)
Lan(τ)={−A−τ−eτ/τ−ifτ<0A+τ+e−τ/τ+ifτ≥0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle L^{\mathrm{an}}(\tau)= \begin{cases}-\frac{A_{-}}{\tau_{-}} e^{\tau / \tau_{-}} & \text {if } \tau<0 \tag{S50}\\ \frac{A_{+}}{\tau_{+}} e^{-\tau / \tau_{+}} & \text {if } \tau \geq 0\end{cases}$$\end{document}
whose Fourier transform is given by
(S51)
L^an(ω)=A+1+jτ+ω−A−1−jτ−ω. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{L}^{\mathrm{an}}(\omega)=\frac{A_{+}}{1+j \tau_{+} \omega}-\frac{A_{-}}{1-j \tau_{-} \omega} . \tag{S51} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Again, we can calculate the first order motif coefficients Meian,(10)\begin{document}$M_{e i}^{\mathrm{an},(10)}$\end{document}, Miean,(01)\begin{document}$M_{i e}^{\mathrm{an},(01)}$\end{document} for the antisymmetric rule by substituting Eq. S46 and Eq. S51 in Eq. S45:
(S52)
Meian,(10)=A+τ++τi;Miean,(01)=−A−τ−+τe. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} M_{e i}^{\mathrm{an},(10)}=\frac{A_{+}}{\tau_{+}+\tau_{i}} \quad ; \quad M_{i e}^{\mathrm{an},(01)}=-\frac{A_{-}}{\tau_{-}+\tau_{e}} . \tag{S52} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
These expressions can be included into Eq. S34, leading to the final expression for the symmetric rule:
(S53)
Ψ=αpost+Brinh+MieSy,(01)wexc=αpost+Brinh+(A+τ++τe−A−τ−+τe)wexcw~inh=αprerinh+hexc(αpost+Brinh+(A+τ++τe−A−τ−+τe)wexc)rinh(αpost+Brinh+(A+τ++τe−A−τ−+τe)(wexc−1)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \Psi & =\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{inh}}+M_{i e}^{\mathrm{Sy},(01)} w_{\mathrm{exc}} \\ & =\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{inh}}+\left(\frac{A_{+}}{\tau_{+}+\tau_{e}}-\frac{A_{-}}{\tau_{-}+\tau_{e}}\right) w_{\mathrm{exc}} \tag{S53}\\ \tilde{w}_{\mathrm{inh}} & =\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}+h_{\mathrm{exc}}\left(\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{inh}}+\left(\frac{A_{+}}{\tau_{+}+\tau_{e}}-\frac{A_{-}}{\tau_{-}+\tau_{e}}\right) w_{\mathrm{exc}}\right)}{r_{\mathrm{inh}}\left(\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{inh}}+\left(\frac{A_{+}}{\tau_{+}+\tau_{e}}-\frac{A_{-}}{\tau_{-}+\tau_{e}}\right)\left(w_{\mathrm{exc}}-1\right)\right)} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
And to the final expression for the antisymmetric rule:
(S54)
Ψ=αpost+Brinh+Miean,(01)wexc=αpost+Brinh−A−τ−+τewexcw~inh=αprerinh+hexcαpost+Brinh−A−τ−+τewexcrinh(αpost+Brinh−A−τ−+τewexc−A+τ++τi) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \Psi & =\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{inh}}+M_{i e}^{\mathrm{an},(01)} w_{\mathrm{exc}} \\ & =\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{inh}}-\frac{A_{-}}{\tau_{-}+\tau_{e}} w_{\mathrm{exc}} \tag{S54}\\ \tilde{w}_{\mathrm{inh}} & =\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm{pre}} r_{\mathrm{inh}}+h_{\mathrm{exc}} \alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{inh}}-\frac{A_{-}}{\tau_{-}+\tau_{e}} w_{\mathrm{exc}}}{r_{\mathrm{inh}}\left(\alpha_{\mathrm{post}}+B r_{\mathrm{inh}}-\frac{A_{-}}{\tau_{-}+\tau_{e}} w_{\mathrm{exc}}-\frac{A_{+}}{\tau_{+}+\tau_{i}}\right)} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
